        "problem": "Find $A^4$ given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14048771/assets/45b13bcb5bbd8dc54e605acf6d378f582dcb7a4f2ecfff35a7a5343ee30f0515.png)",
        "worked_solution": "First, to find $A^2,$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
